data/hashing/crc/

vectors.txt holds CRC-32 and CRC-32C test vectors that generate.py wrote on
2026-10-25.  Each line gives a label, CRC-32 as computed by zlib.crc32 of
Python 3, CRC-32C as computed by the crc32c hash of the Linux kernel's crypto
API through an AF_ALG socket, and the message in hexadecimal.  A CRC is written
as the eight hexadecimal digits of its value, the integer whose bytes from the
least significant are the four octets of the check sequence.

The message of the label catalogue is the ASCII string 123456789, whose CRC-32
and CRC-32C are the check values 0xCBF43926 and 0xE3069283 of the CRC catalogue,
https://reveng.sourceforge.io/crc-catalogue/17plus.htm (CRC-32/ISO-HDLC and
CRC-32/ISCSI).  The messages of the label rfc3720 are the five examples of
RFC 3720 Appendix B.4, https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3720, whose CRC-32C
octets the appendix lists.  generate.py checks zlib and the kernel against these
published values before it writes the file.  The messages of the label random
are pseudorandom bytes from Python's random.Random(3720), one message of every
length from 0 to 256 bytes and of the lengths 511, 512, 513, 1000, 1023, 1024,
1025, 4095, 4096, and 4097.

bitwise.txt holds calls of the bitwise CRC-32 of Library/Hashing/Crc/Bitwise
with the results that zlib.crc32 of Python 3 gives, which bitwise.py wrote on
2026-11-09.  Each line names a function, its arguments as sixteen hexadecimal
digits each, and its result as eight: `reg R B RESULT` for crc32Reg, `byte CRC
B RESULT` for crc32Byte, and `word CRC W N RESULT` for crc32Word.  The result of
`byte` is zlib.crc32 of the low byte of B continued from the low 32 bits of CRC,
the result of `word` is zlib.crc32 of the first min(N, 8) bytes of W from the
most significant continued from the low 32 bits of CRC, and the result of `reg`
is the ones complement of zlib.crc32 of the low byte of B continued from the
complement of the register R, which lies below 2^32.  The arguments include CRC
arguments and bytes with high bits set, counts from 0 to beyond 8, pseudorandom
values from Python's random.Random(3720), and the two words of the check string
123456789, whose second call gives 0xCBF43926.  bitwise.py stops if zlib
disagrees with that check value.
